    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что, мы приехали?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well what, we arrived?' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'we've arrived'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, мы на месте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we are in place.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'yes' directly with 'place'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нас уже ждут?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are they waiting for us already?' Notice how 'us' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'are waiting'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Да, они нас ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, they are waiting for us.' Kostya combines 'yes' with 'are waiting' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пошли узнаем, как дела.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go find out how things are.' Listen for the word meaning 'let's go' right alongside 'will find out' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Я немного волнуюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am a little worried.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'am nervous'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ребята, послушайте меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Guys, listen to me.' Max uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'listen'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хочу вам что-то сказать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to tell you something.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'tell'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В последние дни я перегибал палку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the last days I went overboard.' Max links 'last' directly with 'day' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я вел себя очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I behaved very badly.' Max combines 'I' with 'badly' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Мы понимаем тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We understand you.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'we' with 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спасибо вам за поддержку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you for the support.' Max uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'support', keeping a formal or respectful register. Now let us listen to the conversation again without interruption.",
